CAT = "cpaiscInfra"; SEG = "INF"
Q = [
("A company rents virtual machines from a provider, installs its own operating system and runs its own applications on them. Which cloud service model is this?",
 "IaaS", ["PaaS", "SaaS", "Hybrid cloud"],
 "NIST SP 800-145 defines IaaS as the consumer controlling operating systems, storage and deployed applications while the provider manages the underlying infrastructure. In PaaS the provider also runs the operating system."),
("Developers deploy their code onto a provider's managed runtime and database and never touch the operating system. Which service model is this?",
 "PaaS", ["IaaS", "SaaS", "Private cloud"],
 "In PaaS the consumer controls its deployed applications and possibly their configuration, but not the servers, operating systems or storage. That is the line between PaaS and IaaS."),
("Under SaaS, what does the customer typically still control?",
 "Limited user-specific settings", ["Operating system patch schedule", "Network firewall rule sets", "Application source code changes"],
 "NIST SP 800-145 says a SaaS consumer manages nothing beneath the application except, possibly, limited user-specific configuration settings. Patching and network rules sit with the provider."),
("Cloud infrastructure provisioned for the exclusive use of several organizations with shared security and compliance concerns is a:",
 "Community cloud", ["Public cloud", "Private cloud", "Virtual private cloud"],
 "A community cloud serves a specific community with shared concerns. A private cloud serves a single organization, and a public cloud is open to the general public."),
("A company runs payroll in its private cloud and moves peak workloads to a public provider using technology that ports the application between them. This deployment is a:",
 "Hybrid cloud", ["Community cloud", "Public cloud", "Private cloud"],
 "A hybrid cloud joins two or more distinct cloud infrastructures that stay separate but are bound by technology allowing data and application portability, such as cloud bursting."),
("A user adds storage through a web portal without contacting the provider's staff. Which essential cloud characteristic is this?",
 "On-demand self-service", ["Rapid elasticity", "Resource pooling", "Measured service"],
 "On-demand self-service means provisioning capabilities without human interaction with the provider. Rapid elasticity is scaling up and down quickly, often automatically."),
("A provider serves many customers from shared physical servers, assigning capacity dynamically as demand changes. Which characteristic is this?",
 "Resource pooling", ["Broad network access", "On-demand self-service", "Measured service"],
 "Resource pooling is the multi-tenant model where physical and virtual resources are assigned and reassigned to consumers by demand."),
("Under the shared responsibility model for IaaS, who is normally responsible for physical security of the data center?",
 "Cloud service provider", ["Cloud customer", "Customer's internal audit", "Customer and provider jointly"],
 "In every service model the provider runs the physical facilities. Customers keep more responsibility as they move from SaaS to IaaS, but never the building."),
("In a PaaS arrangement, what does the customer control?",
 "Its deployed applications' settings", ["Host operating system builds", "Underlying network design", "Physical storage arrays"],
 "NIST SP 800-145 says a PaaS consumer controls its deployed applications and possibly configuration settings for the hosting environment, but not the network, servers, operating systems or storage."),
("Which IT component allocates memory and processor time to the programs running on a computer?",
 "Operating system", ["Database management system", "Network switch", "Load balancer"],
 "The operating system manages hardware resources and schedules programs. A DBMS manages data, and switches and load balancers move network traffic."),
("Which device forwards traffic between separate networks using IP addresses?",
 "Router", ["Switch", "Wireless access point", "Network interface card"],
 "A router connects different networks and chooses paths by IP address. A switch connects devices within one local network using hardware addresses."),
("Software that lets one physical server run several isolated virtual machines is a:",
 "Hypervisor", ["Container image", "Middleware layer", "Load balancer"],
 "A hypervisor creates and runs virtual machines, each with its own operating system. Containers share the host operating system instead."),
("Which device spreads incoming web requests across several servers so no single server is overloaded?",
 "Load balancer", ["Router", "Domain controller", "Intrusion detection system"],
 "A load balancer distributes requests among servers, which supports availability. A router chooses network paths but does not balance work among servers."),
("Processing sensor data on devices near where it is created, rather than sending it all to a central data center, is:",
 "Edge computing", ["Grid computing", "Mainframe processing", "Batch processing"],
 "Edge computing moves processing close to the data source to cut delay and bandwidth. Grid computing pools many machines to work on one large task."),
("Why does a company that outsources hosting to a cloud provider obtain the provider's SOC 2 report?",
 "To evaluate controls it relies on", ["To get an opinion on its own ICFR", "To test its own user entity controls", "To certify the provider under PCI DSS"],
 "A SOC 2 report describes and gives an opinion on the provider's controls over security and the other categories, so customers can judge the controls they depend on. It covers none of the customer's own controls and is not a PCI certification."),
("Which pricing approach reflects the measured service characteristic of cloud computing?",
 "Paying for resources actually used", ["Fixed perpetual software license", "Buying servers before deployment", "One flat fee for each site"],
 "Measured service means resource use is metered and reported, which supports pay-per-use billing. Buying hardware or perpetual licenses is the on-premise model."),
]
